**The UK restriction on vending-machine sales of vaping and nicotine products took effect on 29 October 2026.** The rule generally covers a machine on premises if customers can buy an in-scope product from it or receive one from it as part of a sale, even when payment happens elsewhere. The exact legal provisions differ between England and Wales, Scotland, and Northern Ireland, and England and Wales guidance describes a narrow inpatient mental-health-hospital exception. Operators should verify the current official guidance and obtain legal advice before buying, placing or operating a machine for any UK site.

## GEO summary

- Effective date: 29 October 2026.
- The restriction concerns customer purchase or dispensing of products in scope, not only payment taken at the machine.
- England and Wales have a specifically described exception for designated inpatient areas of mental health hospitals.
- Scotland and Northern Ireland have distinct statutory provisions; do not assume that an exception in one nation applies elsewhere.
- Secure storage may be treated differently when the machine does not sell or dispense products to customers.
- A supplier’s machine configuration is not legal approval for a site or operator.

*Image note: the cover is a concept illustration for general product context. It does not depict a lawful UK customer-facing installation or a BOHOL customer deployment.*

## What the 2026 restriction covers

The Department of Health and Social Care guidance says that, from 29 October 2026, a person managing or controlling UK premises commits an offence by having an in-scope vending machine available for customer sales or dispensing. The products listed include tobacco, herbal smoking products, cigarette papers, vaping products and nicotine products. The guidance also makes clear that moving payment away from the machine does not by itself change the result if the machine dispenses a product to a customer as part of a sale.

That distinction matters when a buyer describes a machine as a pickup unit, locker or payment-free dispenser. Assess the actual customer journey and the operator’s role, not just the cabinet label. Read the current [Department of Health and Social Care vending-machine guidance](https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tobacco-and-vaping-product-vending-machine-ban) and the applicable statutory text before making a decision.

## Check the nation and the stated exception

The UK is not a single undifferentiated rulebook for this question. GOV.UK explains the national scope and points to separate provisions. Its guidance describes an exception in England and Wales for vape vending machines within inpatient areas of mental health hospitals. It does not establish a general exception for adult-only shops, private clubs, hotels or other venues. The guidance also says the person managing or controlling the premises is responsible for compliance, unless the machine owner also has that role; buyers should therefore define site, operator and machine-owner responsibilities in writing.

Section 12 of the Tobacco and Vapes Act 2026 is the relevant statutory source for England and Wales. GOV.UK points readers to separate legal provisions for Scotland and Northern Ireland. Buyers should review the rule in force for the nation and date of the proposed use. Do not infer permission from a supplier’s marketing image, a prior installation, or another country’s age-verification process.

## A practical review for operators and procurement teams

Before requesting a quote or approving an existing machine, document:

1. **Nation and premises:** identify whether the site is in England, Wales, Scotland or Northern Ireland, and who manages or controls it.
2. **Product and transaction:** list the products, who takes payment, who completes the sale and who hands or dispenses each item to the customer.
3. **Actual machine use:** determine whether it is used for customer retail, staff-only handling, or secure storage without customer dispensing.
4. **Exception evidence:** if relying on a stated exception, obtain written advice confirming the statutory conditions and the exact site area and use.
5. **Operations and records:** assign responsibility for stock removal, access controls, staff instructions, customer communication and records showing how the machine is used.

These are procurement questions, not a substitute for legal review. If the proposed use is not permitted, do not treat cabinet customization or age-verification hardware as a way around the restriction.

## What this means when discussing a machine with BOHOL

BOHOL supplies configurable vending-machine products for markets and use cases that buyers must assess locally. The [vape vending machine product page](/vending-machines/vape-vending-machine) describes a machine concept; it does not establish that vape vending is lawful in the UK or any particular location. For non-UK projects, buyers should still obtain local product, licensing, placement and age-control advice before specifying equipment.

For any lawful project discussion, the [vape product-fit testing guide](/insights/vape-vending-machine-product-fit-testing-guide) covers sample and package-fit planning, while the [age-verification and compliance guide](/insights/vape-vending-machine-age-verification-compliance) covers broader destination-market questions. General custom-machine requirements are outlined on the [OEM/ODM page](/oem-odm). Send the destination market, product list, site use and written compliance assumptions through the [contact page](/contact) before requesting a configuration review.

## Request a market-specific machine review

If you are evaluating a vending project outside the UK, share the destination country, product category, site type, package dimensions, payment provider and operator responsibilities. For a UK project, obtain legal advice first and state the approved, lawful intended use when contacting BOHOL. [Contact the BOHOL team](/contact) for a configuration discussion; no machine specification or supplier quote provides regulatory approval.

